{
  "dom_id": "step-q98666",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Find the radius of the osculating circle at the point where $t=1$ for the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩.$",
  "worked_solution": "Since we are given only the curve $r(t)$, we will use the cross product formula to find the radius of curvature:\n\n$$\nR(t)=\\frac{1}{κ(t)}=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t),$ ${r}^{′}(1)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{″}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(1)$ and ${r}^{″}(1)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\times ⟨0,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the radius of the osculating circle at  $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}}{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}=\\frac{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}{\\,1\\,}=2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
